{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on 'probable outcomes', where an outcome is considered probable if its probability is higher than 1 divided by the number of outcomes. Lottery A has 2 outcomes (6000€ with 25%, 0€ with 75%), so 1/2 = 0.5. Thus, any outcome with probability > 0.5 is considered probable. Only the 0€ outcome has 75% (which is > 0.5), so 6000€ (25%) is not probable. Therefore, only the 0€ outcome is probable in A. Lottery B has 3 outcomes: 4000€ (25%), 2000€ (25%), 0€ (50%). 1/3 ≈ 0.333. So outcomes with probability > 0.333 are probable. 4000€ (25%): 25% < 33.3% → not probable; 2000€ (25%): same → not probable; 0€ (50%): 50% > 33.3% → probable. Thus, only the 0€ outcome is probable in B. Both lotteries have only one probable outcome (0€), so the average number of probable outcomes is 1 for both. Therefore, the reason is indifferent between A and B. However, the reason prefers the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes — if both are equal, it is indifferent. Thus, the reason applies and is applicable to this decision problem.",
